n relationship — Disputed genealogical proposal; no consensus implied"><outline text="Robbeets et al. (2021): Under the Transeurasian hypothesis, Japonic, Koreanic, Tungusic, Mongolic and Turkic are branches of one proposed genealogical group." url="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04108-8" publication="Nature article" /><outline text="Tian et al. (2022): This critique argues that the linguistic, genetic and archaeological analyses do not provide conclusive evidence for that family or its proposed farming dispersal. This is rejection of the evidence, not a demonstrated alternative superfamily." url="https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.06.09.495471" publication="bioRxiv preprint, version 1" /><outline text="Robbeets et al. (2022): The response revises datasets and approaches while defending the proposed relationship and the triangulation argument." url="https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.10.05.510045" publication="bioRxiv preprint, version 1" /></outline><outline text="Bolgar" node-id="bolg1249" source="glottolog"><outline text="Bolgarian" node-id="bolg1250" source="glottolog" /><outline text="Chuvash · Чăваш чĕлхи" node-id="chuv1255" source="glottolog"><outline text="Conditional placements"><outline text="According to Glottolog 5.3: Bolgar" url="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18840935" node-ref="bolg1249" /><outline text="According to WALS Online / CLDF: Turkic" url="https://wals.info" node-ref="wals:genus-turkic" /><outline text="According to Hruschka et al. (2015): Clade: Rumelian Turkish, Bashkir, Chuvash …" url="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cub.2014.10.064" node-ref="ph:hruschka_et_al2015:1" /></outline><outline text="Anatri" node-id="anat1247" source="glottolog" /><outline text="Viryal" node-id="viry1239" source="glottolog" /></outline></outline><outline text="Common Turkic" node-id="comm1245" source="glottolog"><outline text="Oghuz" node-id="oghu1243" source="glottolog"><outline text="Nuclear Oghuz" node-id="nucl1769" source="glottolog"><outline text="East Oghuz" node-id="e