et al. (2021): Under the Transeurasian hypothesis, Japonic, Koreanic, Tungusic, Mongolic and Turkic are branches of one proposed genealogical group." url="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04108-8" publication="Nature article" /><outline text="Tian et al. (2022): This critique argues that the linguistic, genetic and archaeological analyses do not provide conclusive evidence for that family or its proposed farming dispersal. This is rejection of the evidence, not a demonstrated alternative superfamily." url="https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.06.09.495471" publication="bioRxiv preprint, version 1" /><outline text="Robbeets et al. (2022): The response revises datasets and approaches while defending the proposed relationship and the triangulation argument." url="https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.10.05.510045" publication="bioRxiv preprint, version 1" /></outline><outline text="Jejueo" node-id="jeju1234" source="glottolog"><outline text="Conditional placements"><outline text="According to Glottolog 5.3: Koreanic" url="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18840935" node-ref="kore1284" /><outline text="According to Lee (2015): Clade: Jollado, Jejueo" url="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0128448" node-ref="ph:lee2015:21" /></outline></outline><outline text="Korean · 한국어" node-id="kore1280" source="glottolog"><outline text="Conditional placements"><outline text="According to Glottolog 5.3: Koreanic" url="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18840935" node-ref="kore1284" /><outline text="According to WALS Online / CLDF: Korean" url="https://wals.info" node-ref="wals:genus-korean" /><outline text="According to Hartmann &amp; Walkden (2024): Clade: Korean, Japanese" url="https://doi.org/10.16995/glossa.10598" node-ref="ph:hartmann_and_walkden2024:105" /></outline><outline text="Chungchong-Central Korean" node-id="chun1248" source="glottolog"><outline text="Conditional placements"><outline text="According to Glottolog 5.3: Korean" url="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18840935" node-ref="kore1280